course: dictionary
lesson: 8933
title: Significant: important, or with a special meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Significant» usually means important, or large enough to matter. You will meet it in maths, science and history. In history, a significant event is one that changed things. The clues are in the words around it: «The invention was significant because it changed how people travelled.» If it changed how people travelled, it must be important.
explain: The same word can mean different things in different places. In maths, «significant figures» are the digits that matter in a number for rounding: «4 586 to 2 significant figures is 4 600.» In science, a «significant result» is one that is not just chance. Always check the subject you are reading about.
explain: Sometimes «significant» means having a special meaning. «She gave him a significant look across the room.» No words were said, but the look told him something. To check, swap in your meaning, such as «important» or «with a special message», and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Significant means important, or worth noticing.
fact: Significant can also mean large enough to matter, as in «a significant amount».
fact: In maths, significant figures are the digits that matter in a number for rounding: «4 586» to 2 significant figures is 4 600.
fact: In science, a significant result is one that is not just chance.
fact: «Significant other» means partner (polite, US).
exercise: «The new rule made a significant change to how we line up for lunch.» What does significant mean here? | Written in red | *Important, large enough to matter | Quick and noisy | Tiny and not worth noticing
exercise: In maths, what are significant figures? | *The digits that matter in a number for rounding | Shapes drawn with a ruler | Numbers that are always even | The pages at the back of a maths book
exercise: «4 586 to 2 significant figures is 4 600.» What does 4 600 show? | The number of children in a class | The answer to a sum of 4 and 6 | The page number of the book | *The number rounded to 2 significant figures
test: «A significant result is one that is not just chance.» What does «a significant difference» suggest in a science test? | Nothing important at all | *A difference that is not just chance | A difference in the size of the paper | A difference in colour
test: «Significant other» is a polite US phrase. What does it mean? | Teacher | *Partner | Neighbour | Pet
exam: «The invention was significant because it changed how people lived.» What does significant mean? | Happy and loud | Small and easy to miss | *Important, because it changed things | Late in the day
